# Example Caddy config for prodbox. Caddy is the only thing facing the internet: the
# container port is bound to 127.0.0.1, and Caddy runs on the host (not inside
# docker/deploy/compose.deploy.yml), terminating TLS and reverse-proxying to the
# loopback port this stack's container published.
#
# Copy into /etc/caddy/Caddyfile alongside the pitchbox repo's own blue-green Caddy
# config - the two are independent, unrelated blocks - and reload Caddy. The host and
# port below are already this stack's real values, nothing to replace.
#
# landing.pitchbox.app is this stack's first deployed host, not the apex. The apex
# (pitchbox.app) and www.pitchbox.app keep pointing at the pitchbox repo's own
# blue-green stack until #425 builds this landing's real pages; that vhost flip is
# deliberately out of scope here, so this file does not configure pitchbox.app or
# www.pitchbox.app.
#
# The DNS record must be grey-clouded (Cloudflare: DNS-only, proxy switch off) rather
# than orange-clouded, or Caddy cannot complete its own ACME challenge and terminate
# TLS directly:
#
#   landing.pitchbox.app   A/AAAA  <prodbox IP>   ; grey-clouded
#
# 127.0.0.1:5284 is reserved for a second stack under this repo (not yet built - see
# docker/deploy/secrets.env.example); when it exists it gets its own host block here,
# its own subdomain, and its own grey-clouded DNS record, the same shape as this one.

landing.pitchbox.app {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5184
}
